Run the fs requests that cannot be cancelled anyway on the loop thread, and
deliver their completions from the pending phase, one batch per loop turn.

The threadpool exists to keep a blocking disk from stalling the event loop.
Here there is no disk: every fs syscall is answered by the Sentry in
microseconds, on the same thread the caller is already on. Paying a threadpool
round trip for that costs a measured 304 us per async lstat against 47 us for
the synchronous one -- five extra kernel crossings per request (two futex, an
epoll wait, an eventfd write, a drain read) and a wait in uv__io_poll that on a
tar extract of one npm package adds up to seven seconds of idle JS thread.

So POST grows a third arm. For an eligible request it runs uv__fs_work on this
thread and then publishes the struct uv__work in exactly the state a worker
thread would have left it in -- work == NULL, queued on the tail of loop->wq
under loop->wq_mutex -- so uv__work_done, uv__fs_done and everything above them
run unchanged and do not know which thread did the work.

What it does not do is call the callback. node's AsyncDestCall touches the
FSReqBase after ReqWrap::Dispatch returns, so a callback that ran inside
uv_fs_lstat() would see a half-built wrap; the guarantee that no user code runs
before the submitting call returns is load-bearing above libuv, not an
implementation detail of the pool.

Nor does it call uv_async_send(). That function's eventfd write exists to
interrupt a uv__io_poll() sleeping on another thread, and there is no other
thread here: the write and the epoll wake it buys would both be paid on the
loop thread with nothing offloaded in exchange. Measured, that arm is slower
than the threadpool it replaces (a serial async lstat: 327.7 us against the
pool's 294.3 us). What replaces it is a plain counter,
uv__loop_internal_fields_t::inline_pending, incremented under nothing in
particular because only the loop thread ever touches it, and read in two
places in unix/core.c:

  - uv__backend_timeout() returns 0 while it is non-zero, so uv__io_poll()
    polls and does not sleep on a completion that is already sitting on
    loop->wq. This is the pending_queue test beside it, for a queue
    uv__io_poll() cannot see.
  - uv_run() clears it and calls uv__work_done(&loop->wq_async) immediately
    after uv__run_pending(), in the pending phase, before idle, prepare and
    the poll.

The counter is cleared *before* uv__work_done() runs, and that is the whole
design. uv__work_done() drains everything sitting on loop->wq, so a turn
delivers a batch and not a single completion; what the early clear guarantees
is that a request submitted inline from inside a completion callback re-arms
the counter and always lands in the *next* batch, delivered by the next turn's
pending phase rather than the rest of this one -- so a chain of dependent fs
requests costs one batch per turn, with a callback-submitted request always in
the following one, and with uv__run_check() and every setImmediate in it
running between each pair, exactly as it does when a worker thread services
the chain.

Feeding loop->async_io_watcher with uv__io_feed() instead would not hold that
line: uv_run() re-runs uv__run_pending() up to eight more times after
uv__io_poll() returns, so a completion fed from inside a callback is drained
in the same turn and up to nine dependent requests finish before the check
phase. node's own test-fs-promises-file-handle-readFile sees exactly that. Its
"Signal aborted right before buffer read" case races a setImmediate-scheduled
AbortController against the fstat/read pair inside readFileHandle, and it
needs the abort to land between them.

Which is why READ and WRITE are inline here too, and why they have to be.
Leaving them on the pool would put a dependent read in the poll phase of the
turn that delivered its fstat -- one turn for two requests again, by the other
route. Both go inline, and the chain becomes: turn 1 delivers the open and the
callback submits the fstat; turn 1's check phase runs the abort's setImmediate;
turn 2 delivers the fstat and the callback submits the read; turn 3 delivers
the read into a checkAborted that rejects.

READ and WRITE cannot be inlined unconditionally, though: a read on a pipe, a
socket or a tty blocks, and blocking the loop thread is the one thing this
change must not do. So an fd is eligible only if it is known to be a regular
file, and the knowing costs no per-op syscall. uv__fs_open_register() fstats
the fd once at the completion of a UV_FS_OPEN and stores S_ISREG(st.st_mode)
in one byte of uv__fs_regular_fd[]. The store is unconditional: a successful
open of a pipe, a socket or a tty -- or an fstat that fails -- writes 0, so
the byte is authoritative at every open rather than merely set on regular
files, and a recycled fd number cannot inherit a stale 1 from whatever held it
last. uv_fs_close() clears the byte before the close, on both arms and on the
synchronous path too. An fd that never came out of a uv_fs_open() at all --
stdin, stdout, stderr, a pipe, a socket, anything opened outside libuv -- has
no byte set and stays on the pool, which is the safe default rather than a
special case.

Every open uv__fs_work runs registers, whichever arm ran it: the call is at the
end of uv__fs_work, so the inline, pool and synchronous opens all pay it.
fs.openSync() followed by asynchronous fs.read() is an ordinary node shape --
readFileSync's neighbours in lib/fs.js do it, and so does anything that opens a
file up front and streams it afterwards -- and an fd whose byte is unset would
sit on the threadpool for no reason at all, since the fd is just as much a
regular file for having been opened synchronously. The synchronous arm
therefore pays one extra fstat per fs.openSync(): the same single syscall the
asynchronous arm pays, on a path that was already making a blocking open on
this thread. A pool open registers too, so its fd does not inherit the byte a
previous regular file left under that number. The fstat now sits inside the
census's sync bracket on the synchronous arm and outside the syscall bracket on
the other two, which moves its time between census rows and nowhere else.

That table is a file-static in unix/fs.c and deliberately not a member of
uv__loop_internal_fields_t, because a close cannot always reach a loop: node
calls uv_fs_close(nullptr, &req, fd, nullptr) in fourteen places, which is
legal for a synchronous request and which uv__get_internal_fields() cannot
survive -- a loop-keyed table would be unclearable in exactly the places
clearing matters, and dereferencing the null loop to clear it segfaults node
during startup. The fd space is per process in any case, since worker threads
run their own loops over the same fds, so the table is per process too: a
fixed 65536 bytes, indexed by fd, with anything above that range left
untracked and therefore on the pool. The bytes are relaxed atomics because two
loop threads may touch them at once; nothing orders on them, and a lost race
can only make an fd look untracked, which costs a threadpool round trip and
nothing else.

OPEN itself is the same hazard one step earlier: open(2) of a FIFO blocks until
the other end is opened, and a process that opens both ends of one -- node's
test-http2-respond-file-with-pipe does, with respondWithFile reading the FIFO
and fs.writeFile filling it -- would block its only loop thread in the first
open and never reach the second. So uv__fs_open_may_block() stats the path
first, and an open goes inline only when the path is a regular file, a
directory or a path stat cannot resolve (the open then fails at once, or
O_CREAT makes a regular file), or when O_NONBLOCK says the open cannot wait;
anything else goes to the pool, where upstream opens it. That is one stat per
asynchronous open -- an ENOENT one for every file a tar extraction creates --
which the lstat figures above predate. It is a check and not a guarantee: a
path replaced by a FIFO between the stat and the open still blocks, which is
the price of not intercepting open(2) in the kernel.

The rest of the op set is not a judgement call either: it is known_broken()
from upstream's own test/test-threadpool-cancel.c, the set libuv already
declares non-cancellable when io_uring is servicing it -- close, fdatasync,
fstat, fsync, ftruncate, link, lstat, mkdir, open, rename, stat, symlink,
unlink, and read and write, with open, read and write under the conditions
above. threadpool_cancel_fs skips exactly these, so a request that becomes
uncancellable by running inline breaks no upstream test.

Cancellation follows the io_uring precedent rather than inventing a third
answer. uv__work_cancel's predicate is "still queued AND work != NULL"; an
inline request is queued with work == NULL, so uv_cancel returns UV_EBUSY, the
same answer it gives for a request a worker has already picked up.

Two consequences worth stating. errno is now clobbered by the fs syscall on the
caller's own thread rather than on a worker's -- uv__fs_work sets errno = 0 on
entry and the result is carried in req->result, so libuv itself does not care,
but a caller that read errno across an async uv_fs_* call was reading a
different thread's before and its own now. (The open-time fstat saves and
restores errno for the same reason.) And with every fs request inline the
threadpool may never be created at all in a process that issues no other work;
uv__work_cancel and uv__work_submit both still run uv_once(&once, init_once)
before touching the global mutex, so nothing observes an uninitialised pool.

The fd table's bit is set by uv_fs_open() and cleared by uv_fs_close(), and
those are the only two writers -- which is the hazard, stated plainly rather
than left for a reader to find. An fd that uv_fs_open() opened on a regular
file and that is then closed by something other than uv_fs_close() -- a bare
close(2), or libuv's own uv__close(), both of which node does use -- leaves
its bit set. The kernel is free to hand that same fd number back for the next
open of anything at all, including a pipe or a socket; if that reopen goes
through uv_fs_open() the unconditional store corrects the byte, but if it does
not -- a bare open(2), an accept(4), a pipe(2) -- the table still says
"regular file" until something calls uv_fs_close() on it. A uv_fs_read()
on such an fd then takes the inline path and blocks the loop thread inside
read(2) until the peer writes or closes: not a wrong answer, but the one thing
this change exists to avoid, and invisible in a census because the time lands
in the caller's own frame. Nothing in node reaches that shape today -- every
fd node opens through uv_fs_open() it closes through uv_fs_close() -- so this
is a constraint on future callers, not a live bug. Widening the table to
observe every close would mean intercepting close(2) itself, which is a
kernel-side change, not a libuv one.

VISORJS_UV_FS_INLINE selects the arm, read once on the first async request the
way uv__use_io_uring reads UV_USE_IO_URING. Default on; "0" is off and is
semantically upstream, which makes it the A/B arm. Semantically, not
byte-for-byte: with "0" the added code is still compiled in -- the extra
branch in POST, uv__loop_internal_fields_t::inline_pending, uv__fs_regular_fd[]
and its clear in uv_fs_close() are all there -- it is only that uv__fs_inline()
returns 0 and no request ever takes the inline path, nor pays the pre-open
stat. uv__fs_open_register() is not gated on it, so an "0" run still pays the
one fstat per open. Any other value is on.

The split is a #if defined(__wasm__) rather than an unconditional change only
because deps/uv/uv.gyp builds libuv for both toolsets: node_js2c links a host
copy built by the host compiler, which has no Sentry underneath it and for which
the threadpool is exactly right. That branch is upstream's, unmodified --
inline_pending is not even a member of the host copy's internal fields, and
uv__fs_regular_fd[] is not compiled into it.

